-- title:
--   Right-ideal depth $D_R(M)$
-- statement:
--   For a monoid $M$ and $m\in M$, the principal right ideal generated by $m$ is $mM=\{ms:s\in M\}$. The **right-ideal depth** $D_R(M)$ is the length of the longest chain of principal right ideals
--
--   $$m_0M\subsetneq m_1M\subsetneq\cdots\subsetneq m_kM,$$
--
--   counted as the number $k$ of strict containments; for finite $M$ it is at most $|M|-1$.
--
--   It is the parameter of the log-free bound of Theorem 17 for $\mathcal R$-trivial monoids.
--
--   **Formalization Note** A natural-number supremum over the lengths of chains, indexed by $\{0,\dots,k\}$. The set of lengths contains $0$ and, for a finite monoid, is bounded, so the supremum is attained. For an infinite monoid with unbounded chains it would be the junk value $0$; every statement using $D_R$ assumes $M$ finite. The right ideal is written as a set, as in the published $\mathcal R$-triviality predicate.
-- source:
--   Lee–Santha, The quantum query complexity of the semigroup product problem, arXiv:2609.39974 (held version, sha256 9eec405d…ffb6), §3, p.23

import Mathlib

namespace MonoidProdQ.Supplement

/-- Lee–Santha, *The quantum query complexity of the semigroup product problem*,
arXiv:2609.39974 (held version, sha256 9eec405d…ffb6), §3, p.23: "Define D_J(M) to be the length of
the longest chain Mm₁M ⊊ Mm₂M ⊊ ⋯ ⊊ MmₖM of principal two-sided ideals, counted as the number of
strict containments k − 1 ≤ |M| − 1. Similarly D_R(M) denotes the length of the longest chain of
(principal) right ideals." This is `D_R(M)`: the largest `k` such that there are
`m₀, …, mₖ ∈ M` with `m₀M ⊊ m₁M ⊊ ⋯ ⊊ mₖM`.

**Formalization Note.** The right ideal `mM` is `Set.range (fun s => m * s)`, as in the published
`MonoidProdQ.IsRTrivial`. A chain of `k` strict containments has `k + 1` members, indexed by
`Fin (k + 1)`. The value is a natural-number supremum; the set contains `0` (one-member chains)
and, for a finite monoid, is bounded by `|M| − 1` (the members of a strict chain are distinct
sets), so the supremum is attained. For an infinite monoid with unbounded chains `sSup` would be
the junk value `0`; every statement using `D_R` assumes `M` finite. -/
noncomputable def rightIdealDepth (M : Type*) [Monoid M] : ℕ :=
  sSup {k : ℕ | ∃ m : Fin (k + 1) → M, ∀ i : Fin k,
    Set.range (fun s : M => m i.castSucc * s) ⊂ Set.range (fun s : M => m i.succ * s)}

end MonoidProdQ.Supplement


